    "body": "## Summary\n\nImplements **ADR-023: Skip-Subtree Optimization for Hover** — wrapper-element hovers now cascade only hover-sensitive descendants instead of every element in the subtree.\n\nThree commits. **Detection works (63/63 transitions were Level 1), link hovers unchanged, structural hovers improved 2.2× (900ms → 400ms).** But the delivered number is **20-40× short of the ADR's prediction** (~20ms). This PR documents that honestly, names the root cause, and proposes the specific followup.\n\n## What shipped\n\n| Hover type | ADR-022 baseline | ADR-023 measured | Change |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Level detection** | — | **`level=1`** (all 63 transitions) | ✅ works correctly |\n| Link hover (~30 transitions) | 14-25ms | **14-25ms** | ✅ unchanged |\n| Medium element (~10 transitions) | 55-67ms | **22-40ms** | ~1.7× faster |\n| **Structural element (~23 transitions)** | **850-932ms** | **340-460ms** | ✅ **~2.2-2.5× faster** |\n\n**Algorithmic guard confirms the mechanism:** synthetic document (1 wrapper `<div>`, 1000 `<span>`, 10 `<a>`) — `apply_rule` calls dropped from ~17,187 (full cascade) to **170** (filtered, = 10 `<a>` × 17 rules). The filtered walk skips the 1000 `<span>` descendants entirely.\n\n## What the spec missed (honest)\n\nADR-023's Decision section predicted wrapper hovers would drop to **~8-20ms**. Measured: **340-460ms**. Off by 20-40×.\n\n**Root cause:** scale-estimate error, same class as ADR-021 → ADR-022's prediction miss.\n\n- Synthetic test document: **10 `<a>` descendants** per wrapper div\n- Wikipedia article body: **~2,500 `<a>` elements** per wrapper div (references, section links, nav breadcrumbs, footer links)\n\nThe filtered walk correctly skips the non-`<a>` descendants — then cascades all ~2,500 `<a>`s anyway. Full cascade ≈ 133,000 apply_rule calls; filtered ≈ 42,500. **~3× algorithmic reduction**, matching the observed ~2.2-2.5× wall-clock (walk + propagate add ~17ms fixed floor).\n\n## The deeper gap\n\nIn the UA-only case (`a:hover` and nothing else), **none of the `<a>` descendants of a hovered wrapper div actually match `a:hover`.**\n\nPer ADR-018's matcher, `a:hover` requires the `<a>` to be the hovered element OR have the hovered element as an ancestor. `<div>` hovered → no `<a>` matches.\n\nSo the **correct** number of elements to re-cascade for a div-hover in UA-only is **zero**. The current implementation cascades ~2,500. Safe (over-cascading preserves cache-equality) but wasteful.\n\nThe algorithmic guard (`div_hover_calls < 1000` with measured 170) verifies that the filtered walk is **skipping non-hover-sensitive elements** — the ADR-023 claim. It does NOT verify that the filtered walk is **skipping elements whose hover match hasn't changed** — a different property, addressed by ADR-024.\n\n## Deferred followup: ADR-024 (hover-match transition check)\n\nThe fix: in `restyle()`'s Level 1 filtered walk, for each flagged element, evaluate `matches_any_anchor_hover(el, old_state)` vs. `matches_any_anchor_hover(el, new_state)`. **Only cascade if they differ.**\n\nFor div-hover in UA-only case, zero elements transition → cascade nothing → ~17ms (propagate 12ms + walk 5ms).\n\n| Hover type | ADR-023 | ADR-024 target |\n|---|---|---|\n| Link hover | 14-25ms | 14-25ms (unchanged) |\n| Wrapper hover | 340-460ms | **~17ms** |\n\nThat's the ~20-30× reduction ADR-023 predicted but didn't deliver.\n\n**Why deferred to ADR-024** rather than extended into ADR-023: the transition check has subtle cache-invalidation implications (must ensure skipped elements' entries stay valid; must ensure cache-equality test still passes for the skip path). Rushed mid-session, it risks a subtle correctness bug that the existing cache-equality tests might miss. Deferred to a clean cycle with dedicated test design.\n\n## Meta-lesson: ADR Followup predictions\n\nBoth **ADR-021 → ADR-022** and **ADR-022 → ADR-023** have the same failure mode: the Followup section predicted a wall-clock number (e.g. \"~8-20ms\") **without measuring the scale of the underlying problem** (how many elements actually get cascaded on real pages). Abstract algorithmic reductions don't translate to wall-clock without knowing the input distribution.\n\n**Going forward** (documented in ADR-023's Meta-lesson section): ADR Followup performance predictions must be either written after measuring the current distribution, or explicitly labeled as unmeasured estimates with a measurement step in the implementation plan.\n\nADR-024's followup will be written *after* this measurement.\n\n## Commits\n\n| Commit | Scope |\n|---|---|\n| `4061761` | `hover_sensitivity.{hpp,cpp}`: `detect_level` + `compute_hover_sensitive`. 6 unit tests. |\n| `65e88a4` | `RenderPipeline` integration + Level dispatch. New algorithmic guard (`170` calls, threshold `<1000`). |\n| `1deb37e` | Docs with measured distribution + ADR-024 followup + Meta-lesson |\n\n## Test count\n\n- Before: 260 cases, 1119 assertions\n- After: **267 cases, 1140 assertions** (+7 cases, +21 assertions)\n- Zero warnings, zero failures\n\n## Verification checklist\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Detection: all Wikipedia transitions Level 1 | ✅ (63/63) |\n| Link hovers unchanged | ✅ (14-25ms) |\n| Structural hovers improved | ✅ (~2.2× faster) |\n| Prediction met (was ~20ms) | ⚠️ **no** — 20-40× miss, documented |\n| Cache-equality invariant preserved | ✅ |\n| Algorithmic guard (skip non-sensitive) | ✅ (170 calls, was ~17,187) |\n\n## Not included (deferred to ADR-024 and beyond)\n\n- **Hover-match transition check** (~400ms → ~17ms for wrapper hovers) — ADR-024 proposed\n- **Level 2 (compound `:hover`) optimization** — no current use case\n- **`prev_hovered_`-aware filtering** — subsumed by ADR-024's transition check\n- **Release build measurements** — current numbers are debug (-O0); release expected 5-10× faster\n\n## ADR\n\n`docs/decisions/023-skip-subtree.md` — status now **Implemented**, with Implementation section documenting the measured distribution + prediction miss, Deferred followup for ADR-024, and Meta-lesson on Followup predictions.\n",
